'''Global University Systems B.V.''' ('''GUS''') is a Netherlands-based [[corporate group]] that owns and operates private [[not-for-profit]] and [[For-profit education|for-profit]] colleges and universities in Europe, North America, Asia, and the Middle East. The company also owns and operates other brands and companies in the education sector.

==History==
The [[London School of Business and Finance]] (LSBF), the forerunner of GUS, was founded in 2003 by British entrepreneur Aaron "Arkady" Etingen.{{r|Guardian1|Companies}} This was expanded to several UK for-profit educational institutions known collectively as the [[LSBF Group]], and including the London College of Contemporary Arts (LCCA) and the Language Gallery (formerly the LSBF School of English).{{r|Guardian1}}{{r|pie1}} After the market reforms of post-secondary education introduced by UK higher education minister [[David Willetts]] in 2011, the LSBF group rapidly expanded its student numbers. Etingen added [[St Patrick's College, London]] to his portfolio when his company Interactive World Wide Limited purchased the college in 2012.{{r|Guardian1|THE1|THE2}} In 2013, LSBF won the [[The King's Awards for Enterprise|Queen's Award for Enterprise]] for its contribution to UK trade.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thepienews.com/lsbf-wins-queens-award-expands-in-canada/|title=LSBF wins Queen’s Award; expands in Canada|first=Dan|last=Thomas|date=April 24, 2013}}</ref>

Etingen established Global University Systems in 2013.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thetimes.com/business/markets/article/topshop-boss-sir-philip-green-back-to-school-with-varsity-deal-with-gus-c6l0tbcnk|title=Topshop boss Sir Philip Green back to school with varsity deal with GUS|first=Oliver|last=Shah|date=August 4, 2018|website=www.thetimes.com}}</ref> Its UK business model relied on access to state-funded loans for student tuition fees, which since 2010 was a model for privatizing English higher education.{{r|Wright}} GUS was incorporated in the Netherlands as a [[Besloten vennootschap met beperkte aansprakelijkheid|Besloten vennootschap]] (or BV) and in turn became the owner of the LSBF Group institutions and St Patrick's College through Dutch holding companies. While BVs are required to produce financial statements and file them with the Dutch [[Kamer van Koophandel|KvK]], the Netherlands has a favourable tax regime compared to the UK.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://business.gov.nl/running-your-business/legal-forms-and-governance/private-limited-company-in-the-netherlands/|title=Private limited company (BV) in the Netherlands|first1=Netherlands Chamber of Commerce|last1=KVK|first2=Statistics Netherlands|last2=CBS|website=business.gov.nl}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Eastwood|first=Noah|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/money/tax/european-country-workers-fleeing-britain-huge-tax-breaks/|title=The European country where British workers are taxed on just 70pc of their income |date=January 15, 2025|work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]|language=en-UK|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250116004154/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/money/tax/european-country-workers-fleeing-britain-huge-tax-breaks/|archive-date=January 16, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> 

This led to further calls in 2017 for scrutiny of Dutch-incorporated private colleges and universities in the UK.{{r|THE3|THE4}} GUS's expansion continued over the next three years with the acquisition of the German business school [[GISMA Business School|GISMA]] (now Gisma University of Applied Sciences)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.gisma.com/blog/gisma-business-school-relaunches-as-gisma-university-of-applied-sciences|title=GISMA Business school relaunches as Gisma University of Applied Sciences|first=Asvin|last=Balloo|date=August 4, 2023|website=Gisma}}</ref> in 2013, [[University Canada West]] in 2014, and [[The University of Law]] in the UK, in 2015. All three were in financial difficulty at the time of their acquisition.{{r|FT1|THE5|LSG1}}

Concerns about the standards at both St Patrick's College and LSBF led to the UK government suspending their [[Points-based immigration system (United Kingdom)#Tier 4|Tier 4 licences]] in 2015.{{r|THE1}}{{r|THE7}}  Following the suspensions, Global University Systems announced plans for a two-year restructuring process of the group's UK holdings to begin in mid-2016. According to John Cox, former director of organisational development at GUS, the plan involved LSBF and its LCCA division coming under a new vocational entity offering only diploma courses, short courses and corporate training products. The vocational courses delivered by St Patrick's College and the Birmingham-based Finance Business Training would also come under this division. The University of Law was to become the sole provider of academic qualifications and professional qualifications, including the master's degrees previously offered by the LSBF Group. However, as of 2019 LSBF was still offering master's degrees awarded by the [[Università telematica internazionale Uninettuno|International Telematic University]] and Geneva Business School. There were no programmes provided or validated by the University of Law.{{r|Pie1|LSBF}}

Between 2016 and 2018 GUS acquired further for-profit education institutions and companies, including the UK-based [[Arden University]] from [[Capella Education]]; [[IBAT College (Swords)|IBAT College Dublin]]; the Israeli test-preparation company HighQ; Israeli college INT college; [[Laureate Education|Laureate Germany]] with its subsidiary institutions University of Applied Sciences Europe and Laureate Academies GmbH;{{r|Times1|THE6|Gueta|SEC}} and R3 Education, a [[Devens, Massachusetts]]-headquartered operator of medical schools in the Caribbean.<ref>https://www.bloomberg.com/profile/company/1333985D:US</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://disclosure.spglobal.com/ratings/en/regulatory/article/-/view/type/HTML/id/2234348|title=S&P Global Ratings|website=disclosure.spglobal.com}}</ref>

In July 2019, Global University Systems bought [[Pearl Academy]] and University of Petroleum and Energy Studies in [[India]] from [[Laureate International Universities]].{{r|BusinessWorld}}

Global University Systems was awarded the title of 'Private Education Group of the Year' by Education Investor Awards 2019.<ref>Education Investor Awards 2019 (4 December 2019) {{usurped|1=[https://web.archive.org/web/20191206105851/https://www.ipevents.net/educationinvestor-awards-2019/winners-2019/ "Winners 2019"]}}. Retrieved 6 December 2019.</ref>

In 2020, GUS expanded its portfolio by acquiring [[Trebas Institute]], a provider of music, film, business, technology, and management programmes with campuses in Toronto and Montreal, Canada.” <ref>{{cite web |url=https://thepienews.com/news/gus-canada-expands-into-montreal/ |title=GUS Canada expands to Montreal |work=The PIE News |date=25 May 2020 |access-date=25 January 2021}}</ref>

In May 2020, GUS expanded its portfolio by acquiring [[Trebas Institute]], a provider of music, film, business, technology, and management programmes with campuses in Toronto and Montreal, Canada.<ref name = "Pie3">''The PIE News'' (25 May 2020), [https://thepienews.com/news/gus-canada-expands-into-montreal "GUS Canada expands to Montreal"]. Retrieved 9 February 2021.</ref>

==Business==
As of 2026, the company states that it has a recruiting network of "over 6,000 recruitment partners" with over "11,000 employees" for its colleges and universities, from more than 175 countries.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.globaluniversitysystems.com/|title=Global University Systems &#124; GUS &#124; Higher Education Partners|website=Global University Systems (GUS)}}</ref> According to [[Moody's Investors Service]], the company was deriving approximately 27% of its revenues in 2018 "from leveraging its agent network and marketing capabilities predominantly to source students for third-party institutions."{{r|Moody1}}

